What are the types of middlewares in Express js ?
=============================================

Application-level middleware – app.use()
Router-level middleware – router.use()
Built-in middleware – express.json(), express.urlencoded(), express.static()
Third-party middleware – cors, morgan, helmet
Error-handling middleware – (err, req, res, next)
1️⃣ Application-level middleware
→ Application level middlewares applies globally to all incoming requests in the entire express js application
→ Applied to the entire app using app.use()
→ Example: logging, authentication, CORS
app.use(loggerMiddleware);

2️⃣ Router-level middleware
→ Applied to specific routes not for all incoming reques using router.use()
→ Helps organize middleware by feature or module
router.use(authMiddleware);


3️⃣ Built-in middleware
→ Provided by Express.js itself
→ Common examples:
express.json()
express.urlencoded()
express.static()
→ We use static for Industry logos but not for this not required authenticatio
project
├── public
│   ├── style.css
│   ├── logo.png
│   └── app.js

app.use(express.static('public'));
http://localhost:3000/logo.png
app.use(express.json());


4️⃣ Third-party middleware
→ Middleware created by external libraries
→ Examples:
cors
morgan
Helmet → use for setting  HTTP security headers
Compression →  use for compression HTTP responses
app.use(cors());


5️⃣ Error-handling middleware
→ It is a special kind of middleware in express used to manage errors happening while handling incoming requests. 
→ Used to handle errors in the application
→ To implement error handling in express, define with four parameters: err, req, res, next here  additionally error object parameter will be use for error handling
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  res.status(500).send('Something went wrong');
});
